question:
if sub-supplier is a cloud service provider (csp):
- do you follow csp security recommendations?
- do you use security dashboard and can you share results?

if sub-supplier is a cloud service provider (csp):
- do you follow csp security recommendations?
- do you use security dashboard and can you share results?

if sub-supplier is a cloud service provider (csp):
- do you follow csp security recommendations?
- do you use security dashboard and can you share results?

if sub-supplier is a cloud service provider (csp):
- do you follow csp security recommendations?
- do you use security dashboard and can you share results?

for projects included or related to the service, does the service provider seek the security validation of the customer before going live for the following?
- cybersecurity risk analysis;
- architecture detailed design review;

on the perimeter of the service, does the service provider implement the following?
- establish a data backup system complying with the needs expressed by the customer;
- conduct monthly monitoring of the implementation of these backup systems;
- protect backups with the same security level applied to the data;
- maintain the same segregation level as the service.
- implement measures to ensure that personal data are protected against accidental destruction or loss.

does the service provider manage and prevent obsolescence of all the software and technical components (e.g.: workstations, servers, network equipment, software, firmware,...) which contribute to the service?
- all software and operating systems installed on or network infrastructure equipment;
- all devices operating software and systems;
- all security software.

does the service provider manage and prevent obsolescence of all the software and technical components (e.g.: workstations, servers, network equipment, software, firmware,...) which contribute to the service?
- all software and operating systems installed on or network infrastructure equipment;
- all devices operating software and systems;
- all security software.

does the service provider respect the following rules?
• does not use a generic or shared id to connect to systems or to development tools. their id must be unique and nominative;
• only uses standard tools and technologies acknowledged and approved by the customer;
• requests that each developer requests validation of his code by another developer in order to detect and correct possible programming mistakes;
• identifies, as much as possible, the components related to the application security from the rest of the code in order to ease their maintenance;
• applies static code analysis tools for security to each and every developed component and remediates any vulnerabilities;
• manages code evolutions. in particular, he creates a new version of the source code for each major modification;
• never uses components such as open-source software and libraries without verifying their quality and compliance with the customer requirements;
• never hard-codes temporary or confidential data – such as passwords or systems addresses;
• eases code maintenance by commenting and documenting it – in particular, security components.
• when testing the code of another developer, a developer must test the components unitarily, then verify their proper functioning after integration;
• makes sure that his tests are comprehensive, covering all use cases. in particular, he verifies the proper functioning of the application in limited cases;
• anticipates the common or probable attacks and verifies the efficiency of the implemented protection measures;
• does not use any critical or strategic production data.
• proceeds to the sanitization of any personal or nominative data. to do so, two major techniques can be used: anonymization and pseudonymization of data. (anonymization irreversibly destroys the relation between the data and the people, and pseudonymization is reversible, usually through a tokenization method).
when tests reveal conception or development defects or non-compliance to specifications, the service provider commits to immediately alert the customer.

does the service provider commit to respect, at his level, the roles and responsibilities segregation principles?
- development activities are non-production activities. developers shall not access production environments;
- during any project, developers commit not to take part in the acceptance testing of components he has developed. likewise, when possible, he does not test the components he has specified;
- developers shall ensure that access rights on systems and applications used during the development phase comply with the least privilege principle.

does the service provider implement the technical and organizational means and measures to cover the security needs of the customer data and ensure that they are not accessible or visible by the following?
- other customers of the service provider;
- employees, co-contractors, or sub-contractors/sub-processors of the service provider.

does the service provider commit to respecting the following rules to protect the information produced through development and comply with the customer's technical norms/thematic policies?
• ensures that the workstations used for development tasks are regularly updated and patched;
• implements protection mechanisms to secure access to code and annex documents – such as configuration files, development tools, and documentation – for example using nominative ids and passwords;
• ensures the maintenance of access rights to code and to source code libraries.
does the service provider commits to respect the professional secrecy and not to disclose any confidential information belonging to the customer?

is the service provider's equipment (e.g., workstations and servers) used for the service protected against attacks, including but not limited to malicious software, through the use of preventive, detective, and recovery measures and controls, according to appropriate standards and policies?
- those protections must be always active and up-to-date.
- where it is not possible to implement these controls and in order to minimize the risk of exposure, the relevant systems must:
- be identified and monitored;
- trigger a security exception process in order to track their non-compliance, and a process concerning the access to the exception list must be shared with the customer soc team to help them during the investigation;
- have their access to the internet and their configuration hardened.

does the service provider have, on the perimeter of the service, a formalized security incident and crisis management process that is compliant with the iso 27035 standard?
with regard to a security incident that either has an impact on
(i) the service; or
(ii) the customer’s information systems; or
(iii) personal data processed by the service provider within the service contract, the service provider shall inform the customer.
is this information communicated within a shorter time as the discovery of the security breach or following the receipt of a complaint by any data subject whose data are processed under the service contract?
does the service provider inform the customer timely of any customer information data loss and the relevant logs for investigative purposes?
this process shall at least include the following:
- detection and recording;
- incident qualification;
- initial support;
- investigation and diagnostic;
- resolution & recovery;
- incident closure.

does the service provider implement the technical and organizational means and measures to cover the security needs of the customer data and ensure that they are not accessible or visible by the following?
- other customers of the service provider;
- employees, co-contractors, or sub-contractors/sub-processors of the service provider.

does the service provider take cybersecurity into account in all its projects, including but not limited to the following?
- incorporate a security-by-design approach;
- cybersecurity risk analysis;
- architecture security review;
- compliance to secure development best practices in order to cover at least the top 10 owasp vulnerabilities.

does the service provider commit to respect, at his level, the roles and responsibilities segregation principles?
- development activities are non-production activities. developers shall not access production environments;
- during any project, developers commit not to take part in the acceptance testing of components he has developed. likewise, when possible, he does not test the components he has specified;
- developers shall ensure that access rights on systems and applications used during the development phase comply with the least privilege principle.

does the service provider manage and prevent obsolescence of all the software and technical components (e.g.: workstations, servers, network equipment, software, firmware,...) which contribute to the service?
- all software and operating systems installed on or network infrastructure equipment;
- all devices operating software and systems;
- all security software.

does the service provider commit to the following?
• to develop all the components implementing data confidentiality protection, data integrity validation, traces and errors management, and operations continuity;
• to adapt the security level of the developed application to the application aict (availability, integrity, confidentiality, traceability) classification, with respect to the customer's guidelines. if no available customer guidelines, developers commit to comply with the applicable best practices (such as the owasp top 10, or asvs - application security verification standard);
• not to add any unspecified component, which could potentially include vulnerabilities or be used as a back-door by malicious individuals;
• to do any development actions based on a formalized software development life cycle (sdlc) process which must define the various stages involved in the development as per the group's security requirements.

